# Luau syntax, tasks and coroutines, signal and budget limits, clocks, reloading

- Luau syntax IS accepted and auto-downleveled to Lua 5.2 before compiling: `+=` (and
  `-=/*=//=/%=/^=/..=`), `continue`, `` `str{}` `` interpolation, if-then-else expressions, and
  type annotations/casts all work. Plain Lua 5.2 is unaffected. Nesting deeper than 200 levels
  is a syntax error.
- `typeof(v)` returns Roblox type names (""Instance"", ""Vector3"", ""EnumItem"", ...). `warn(...)`
  writes to the mod log at Warn level (get_mod_logs shows it). Errors of a one-off execute_lua
  chunk carry no `[mod:...]` prefix.
- What an execute_lua chunk writes with `report()` or `print()` comes back in its result as `Printed`
  (the last 600 characters), next to `Output`, the chunk's return value.
- Signals: `:Connect()`, `:Once()`, and `:Wait()` work; firing queues handlers, and the queue flushes
  at every script-resumption point of the frame loop. `:ConnectParallel()` is `:Connect()` (runs
  serially, logs once per mod). Each handler gets its own copy of a table argument (no metatable).
  A handler that keeps re-firing its own signal is cut after 10 generations (SIGNAL_CASCADE);
  more than 16,384 handler calls of one mod in one resumption point are dropped (BUDGET_EXCEEDED).
- `task.wait/spawn/defer/delay/cancel` work. `task.synchronize/desynchronize` are no-ops that
  log once per mod. `task.wait(math.huge)`, `task.delay(math.huge, f)` and
  `WaitForChild(name, math.huge)` park until cancelled or the mod unloads; a NaN duration is
  BAD_ARGUMENT. `task.cancel` on a finished task does nothing. A `task.defer` from a handler runs
  in the same resumption point.
- `task.spawn/defer/delay` also take a task handle they returned (`task.spawn(t) == t`): a parked
  task resumes, a deferred/delayed one is rescheduled; a finished task, one inside a wait, another
  mod's task, or a `coroutine.create` thread is BAD_ARGUMENT. `coroutine.yield()` inside a task
  parks it until `task.spawn(t, ...)`, whose extra args are what yield returns; inside a handler
  or the main chunk it is CONTEXT_VIOLATION. `task.wait`, `signal:Wait`, `WaitForChild` and `InvokeServer`
  inside a `coroutine.create` coroutine raise CONTEXT_VIOLATION — use `task.spawn` instead.
- `coroutine.resume` only resumes a `coroutine.create` coroutine. Given a task, handler or main-chunk
  thread (a `coroutine.running()` value) it returns `false, ""cannot resume a task or signal-handler
  thread with coroutine.resume; ...""` and leaves the thread alone: resume a parked task with
  `task.spawn(t)`, where `t` is the handle task.spawn/defer/delay returned.
- Budgets: an xpcall handler does not run for a budget cut. Only the code that
  `coroutine.resume`d a `coroutine.create` coroutine that was cut sees `false, err`, and that
  coroutine is dead. A `coroutine.create` body shares the memory budget of the code that resumes it.
  `string.find/match/gmatch/gsub` stop after 5,000,000 matcher steps per call and
  `gsub`/`string.format` results are capped at 1,000,000 chars (both catchable errors). Yielding
  (task.wait, coroutine.yield) inside a `__tostring`, a `table.sort` comparator, a
  `__pairs`/`__ipairs` metamethod, `__index` or a gsub replacement function raises ""attempt to yield
  across a C-call boundary"" (as in Luau); yielding inside pcall is fine. A `coroutine.create` body
  (and a thread task.spawn runs at once) gets at most what the code resuming it has left of steps,
  time and memory. Library functions that call back into your Lua share one cap of 128 levels: a
  pcall/xpcall body or a gsub function counts 1; a `__tostring` via
  tostring/print/warn/string.format, a `table.sort` comparator, `__pairs`/`__ipairs`,
  `coroutine.resume` or an immediate task.spawn counts 2 (pcall nests 128 deep, sort/tostring 63).
  The next raises a catchable ""C stack overflow (...)"". Plain recursion is not limited this way.
- Clocks: `os.time()` (Unix seconds; `os.time({year=,month=,day=[,hour=,min=,sec=]})` reads the
  table as UTC, hour defaults to 12, a field that is not a number counts as missing — a missing
  year/month/day is BAD_ARGUMENT — and a date before 1970 returns nil), `os.clock()`, `time()`
  (scaled game time), and `workspace:GetServerTimeNow()` (Unix seconds every player agrees on: a
  client reads the server's clock once joined, from then on it never goes backwards, and it stands
  still while the server's clock does). `os.date(fmt[, t])` (strftime conversions, `""*t""`) and
  `os.difftime` exist and are UTC with or without `""!""`; `DateTime` is not implemented.
- Standard library = Luau's: `table.find/clear/create/clone/freeze/isfrozen/move/maxn`, `unpack`,
  `string.split`, `string.format` (`%*`, C output: `%d` truncates, `%x` of -1), `utf8.*`, `bit32.*`,
  `math.lerp/map/log10/isnan/isinf/isfinite`, `coroutine.wrap/isyieldable/close`,
  `debug.traceback/info/profilebegin`, `newproxy`, `gcinfo`. Strings hold UTF-16 chars: `#s` counts
  chars, `utf8.len` code points, and utf8 positions are the ones `string.sub` takes. `table.freeze`
  stops library writes (insert, remove, sort, rawset, ...) but not `t.k = v`. One call builds at
  most 1,048,576 elements; `unpack` returns at most 8,000. `buffer`, `vector` and
  `string.pack/unpack` are NOT_IMPLEMENTED (use `string.char`/`string.byte`, `Vector3`);
  `loadstring` returns nil, ""loadstring() is not available""; `setfenv` is refused. `goto`,
  `::labels::` and `_ENV` run here but not in Roblox: do not write them.
- Reloading: `manage_mods reload`, `manage_mods edit` (change a piece of a mod in place: `old_text`
  copied from `get_source`, `new_text`) and the Hub's Save & run all first remove the previous run's
  startup objects — what its main chunk built before its first yield and the mod still owns — so a
  mod that builds its scene at startup never duplicates it; objects made later by handlers, tasks or
  players stay. Pass keep_objects=true to keep everything and build next to it. Two budget cuts in a
  row quarantine the mod and keep it from starting with the next game.
- A player who leaves takes the mods loaded for them along: they are unloaded (after their running
  code returns) and start again on the next world load.

<!-- section: datatypes -->
# Vector3, CFrame, Color3, UDim2, Random, Luau math, argument conversion, Enum

Datatypes are immutable value types; assigning a field errors.
Globals: `Vector3`, `Vector2`, `CFrame`, `Color3`, `UDim`, `UDim2`, `Random`, `Enum`.

- Vector3: `Vector3.new(x,y,z)`, `.zero/.one/.xAxis/.yAxis/.zAxis`,
  `Vector3.FromNormalId(enum)`, `Vector3.FromAxis(enum)`.
  Fields X,Y,Z,Magnitude,Unit. Methods Dot,Cross,Lerp,Angle,FuzzyEq,Abs,Ceil,Floor,Sign,
  Max,Min. Operators + - * / and unary -. `tostring` -> ""x, y, z"".
- Vector2: `Vector2.new(x,y)`, `.zero/.one/.xAxis/.yAxis`; fields X,Y,Magnitude,Unit; same
  methods (Angle included).
- CFrame: `CFrame.new()` (identity), `.new(x,y,z)`, `.new(pos)`, `.new(x,y,z, qx,qy,qz,qw)`,
  12-component `.new(...)`. Also `.identity`, `.lookAt(pos,target[,up])`,
  `.lookAlong(pos,dir[,up])`, `.Angles(rx,ry,rz)` (radians),
  `.fromEulerAngles(rx,ry,rz[,Enum.RotationOrder])` (default XYZ), `.fromEulerAnglesXYZ`,
  `.fromEulerAnglesYXZ`, `.fromOrientation(rx,ry,rz)`, `.fromAxisAngle(axis,angle)`,
  `.fromMatrix(pos,vX,vY[,vZ])`, `.fromRotationBetweenVectors(a,b)`.
  Fields Position, X,Y,Z, Rotation, RightVector, UpVector, LookVector, XVector,YVector,ZVector.
  Methods Inverse, ToWorldSpace, ToObjectSpace, PointToWorldSpace, PointToObjectSpace,
  VectorToWorldSpace, VectorToObjectSpace, Lerp, Orthonormalize, FuzzyEq, GetComponents,
  components, ToEulerAngles([Enum.RotationOrder]), ToEulerAnglesXYZ, ToEulerAnglesYXZ,
  ToOrientation, ToAxisAngle, AngleBetween.
  Operators: `cf * cf`, `cf * v3`, `cf + v3`, `cf - v3`.
- Color3: `Color3.new(r,g,b)` (0..1), `Color3.fromRGB(0..255)`, `Color3.fromHSV(h,s,v)`,
  `Color3.fromHex(""#RRGGBB"")`, `Color3.toHSV(c)`. Fields R,G,B. Methods Lerp, ToHSV, ToHex.
  `tostring`->""r, g, b"".
- UDim: `UDim.new(scale,offset)`; fields Scale,Offset; + - unary-.
- UDim2: `UDim2.new(sx,ox,sy,oy)` or `UDim2.new(udimX,udimY)`, `.fromScale`, `.fromOffset`;
  fields X,Y,Width,Height; method Lerp.
- Random: `Random.new()` or `Random.new(seed)` (deterministic xoshiro).
  `:NextNumber()` -> [0,1); `:NextNumber(min,max)`; `:NextInteger(min,max)`;
  `:NextUnitVector()`; `:Clone()`; `:Shuffle(arrayTable)` (in place, seeded Fisher-Yates).
  NextInteger bounds must be finite whole numbers within +/-9007199254740991.
- Luau math: `math.noise(x, y?, z?)` is Perlin noise (0 at whole-number points, mostly within -1..1,
  repeats every 256; the same inputs give the same value on every machine, so seed terrain with it),
  `math.clamp(x, min, max)` (max below min is an error), `math.round(x)` (halves away from zero) and
  `math.sign(x)` (-1, 0 or 1).

Argument conversion. Both surfaces, the Rbx API and the mod-core functions (`store_set`, `hooks_on`,
`mods_call`, ...), convert arguments and property writes like Luau: a string parameter takes a number as
`tostring` writes it (`store_set(7, 8)` stores ""7"" = ""8""; `FindFirstChild(5)` finds ""5""); a number
parameter takes a string `tonumber` accepts (""0x10"", ""1e3""); an integer parameter truncates toward zero;
a boolean parameter takes only true/false, never a truthy value (`FindFirstChild(name, 1)` is
BAD_ARGUMENT). Datatype constructor numbers default to 0 when nil. UDim/UDim2 offsets are 32-bit
integers (fractions truncate). The mod-core functions report a wrong argument the way Lua does —
`bad argument #1 to 'store_set' (string expected, got table)`.

## Enum

`Enum.<Type>.<Item>` e.g. `Enum.Material.Wood`, `Enum.PartType.Ball`, `Enum.KeyCode.Space`,
`Enum.UserInputType.Keyboard`, `Enum.NormalId.Top`, `Enum.Axis.X`, `Enum.RotationOrder.XYZ`,
`Enum.RaycastFilterType.Exclude`, `Enum.HumanoidStateType.Running`, `Enum.EasingStyle.Quad`,
`Enum.EasingDirection.Out`, `Enum.PlaybackState.Playing`.
Registered enum types: Material, PartType, CameraType, NormalId, Axis, RotationOrder, KeyCode,
UserInputType, UserInputState, MouseBehavior, DevComputerMovementMode, CameraMode,
RaycastFilterType, HumanoidStateType, EasingStyle,
EasingDirection, PlaybackState. Any other `Enum.X` raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED. Item
fields: Name, Value, EnumType. `Enum.GetEnums()`; `Enum.Material:GetEnumItems()`;
`Enum.KeyCode:FromName(""Space"")` / `:FromValue(32)` return the item or nil. Items compare
by identity (`==`). An Enum property or instance-method argument also takes the item's Name or
Value (`part.Material = ""Wood""` or `512`, `UserInputService:IsKeyDown(""E"")`); datatype members
(`TweenInfo.new` easing, CFrame rotation orders, `RaycastParams.FilterType`) need the EnumItem.
Names AND numeric values are the real Roblox ones (e.g. KeyCode gamepad
buttons live at 1000+; all 283 KeyCode items; `Enum.KeyCode.None` is 0 and `Unknown` is its
alias).

<!-- section: instances -->
# Finders, Changed signals, Clone rules, Debris, Model pivots, the service catalog

- Finders: `FindFirstChild(name[,recursive])`, `FindFirstChildOfClass(cls)`,
  `FindFirstChildWhichIsA(cls[,recursive])`, `FindFirstAncestor(name)`,
  `FindFirstAncestorOfClass(cls)`, `FindFirstAncestorWhichIsA(cls)`, `GetChildren()`,
  `GetDescendants()`, `GetFullName()`, `IsA(cls)`, `IsDescendantOf(x)` (nil is BAD_ARGUMENT),
  `IsAncestorOf(x)`. `inst.ChildName` is sugar for an existing child; a missing member errors (use
  FindFirstChild). `WaitForChild(name)` for an absent child yields through the scheduler bridge.
- Properties: Name, ClassName (read-only), Parent, Archivable. Setting Name/Parent/Archivable
  needs WorldEdit. Name keeps its first 100 characters. A Parent deeper than 2,048 levels is
  BAD_ARGUMENT. A Player cannot be destroyed or re-parented (use `player:Kick()`).
- `Instance.new(""Class"", parent)`: the parent argument is deprecated and logs once; set `.Parent` after
  configuring. The instance budget error names the call that went over it.
- `inst.Changed(propertyName)` fires on every instance (a value object passes the new Value);
  `inst:GetPropertyChangedSignal(""Name"")` fires with no args. It refuses an event or method name
  and a typo of a known property (wrong case or one letter off: ""position"" -> ""did you mean
  Position""); a real Roblox property CoreAI does not model yet gets a signal that never fires (logged
  once; the same signal each time you ask for that name, and `Destroy()` disconnects it). Writes by
  a script, a tween or PivotTo fire them (derived members too: a CFrame write fires
  Position/Orientation/Rotation); an equal assignment fires nothing and physics movement never
  does. `AncestryChanged(movedInstance, newParent)` fires on every descendant.
  ChildRemoved/DescendantRemoving and a tag's removed-signal handlers fired by `Destroy()` can
  still read the destroyed instance they were handed (e.g. its Name), as Destroying handlers can.
  `game:IsLoaded()` is true; `game.Loaded` never fires.
- Clone is a deep
  copy: name, attributes, tags and (for parts) Size/CFrame/Color/Anchored/Shape carry across;
  non-Archivable subtrees are skipped and the copy gets fresh ids; `PrimaryPart` and
  `ObjectValue.Value` pointing inside the copy point at the copies, and WorldPivot is kept.
  `game:Clone()`, a service's Clone and `player:Clone()` return nil; `game:Destroy()` errors.
- `Debris:AddItem(inst[, seconds])` (default 10 s) schedules guaranteed destruction without
  yielding and survives the scheduling script's death — the idiom for temporary
  projectiles/effects. WRONG: `task.delay(10, function() part:Destroy() end)` (dies with the
  script); RIGHT: `game:GetService(""Debris""):AddItem(part, 10)`. Past 1,000 queued items the
  oldest is destroyed instantly, so treat the lifetime as a maximum, not an exact delay.
  Needs WorldEdit and write access to the whole subtree; services, `game`, the CurrentCamera and a
  Player are refused (a Player leaves through `player:Kick()`). Re-adding an item replaces its
  lifetime but keeps its place in the eviction order.
- The service catalog has 43 registrations. `HttpService`, `Players`, `Debris`, `TweenService`,
  `CollectionService` and `ScriptContext` are tree-backed (NOT placeholders); `RunService` and
  `UserInputService` have catalog fallbacks that the standard runtime replaces with its live
  implementations (a bare fallback's error names the missing attachment). The standard runtime has
  live tree-backed `Workspace`, `Lighting`, `ReplicatedStorage`, `ServerStorage`,
  `ServerScriptService`, `StarterPlayer`, `StarterGui`, `Players`, `HttpService`, `UserInputService`,
  `RunService`, `MaterialService`, `Debris`, `TweenService` and `CollectionService`, plus
  `UxmlService` (section uxml), `TextChatService` (its `TextChatCommand`s, section chat) and `AIService`
  (answers only when the game turned on AI from Lua, section ai) and `DataStoreService` (section data).
  Placeholders: `ContextActionService` (MVP7),
  `SoundService` (MVP17), and backlog or unsupported Roblox services such as `Teams`,
  `PhysicsService`, `ReplicatedFirst`, `PathfindingService`, `MarketplaceService`, `TeleportService`
  and `BadgeService` (each error says which and gives a workaround). A placeholder's first member
  read, write, or method lookup raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED and names that service's rung, so top-of-file
  acquisition does not abort setup; do not add defensive workarounds solely to avoid that obsolete
  failure. An unknown name raises UNKNOWN_SERVICE with ""X is not a valid Service name"". Also
  `game:FindService(name)`. `workspace` is `game:GetService(""Workspace"")`.

## Model pivots

`model.PrimaryPart` is a BasePart or nil; `model.WorldPivot` is a writable CFrame.
`model:GetPivot()` uses the PrimaryPart CFrame when set, otherwise WorldPivot (initially the
contents' bounding-box center). `model:PivotTo(cf)` rigidly moves every descendant PVInstance.
Pivot/property writes need WorldEdit. BaseParts also support `GetPivot()` and `PivotTo(cf)`
(which moves the part's descendants too), and so does `workspace.CurrentCamera` (a PVInstance).
`model:ScaleTo(factor)` scales every descendant part's size and offset around the pivot; `ScaleTo(1)` restores
the original exactly. `model:GetScale()` returns the factor. On a character it also scales the rig, the body
the character collides with and the first-person eye height. `character:ScaleTo(1.44)` gives a block-game
body: 1.8 blocks tall.

## Error messages

```lua
local ok, err = pcall(function() return Instance.new(""Banana"") end)
if not ok then print(err) end   -- ""BAD_ARGUMENT: Unable to create ... | fix: ... Part ...""

local part = Instance.new(""Part""); part.Parent = workspace
part:Destroy()
local ok2, err2 = pcall(function() return part.Position end)
if not ok2 then print(err2) end  -- ""INSTANCE_DESTROYED: ... | fix: ...""
```

<!-- section: attributes -->
# Attributes, tags, CollectionService, value objects, leaderstats

- `inst:SetAttribute(name, value)` / `inst:GetAttribute(name)` / `inst:GetAttributes()`.
  Value must be string, boolean, number, Vector3, Vector2, Color3, or UDim — anything else is
  rejected with BAD_ARGUMENT. SetAttribute needs WorldEdit. A new attribute name uses only ASCII
  letters, digits, `.`, `-`, `/` and `_` (at most 100 chars, no ""RBX"" prefix); anything else is
  BAD_ARGUMENT.
- `inst:AddTag(t)` / `RemoveTag(t)` / `HasTag(t)` / `GetTags()`. Add/Remove need WorldEdit. A new tag
  is at most 100 characters (BAD_ARGUMENT beyond).
- At most 256 attributes and 256 tags per instance; one more is BAD_ARGUMENT (a world must stay
  savable).
- `CollectionService:AddTag(inst, tag)`, `:RemoveTag`, `:HasTag`, `:GetTags(inst)`,
  `:GetTagged(tag)`, `:GetAllTags()`, plus per-tag `:GetInstanceAddedSignal(tag)` /
  `:GetInstanceRemovedSignal(tag)` and the placewide `.TagAdded` / `.TagRemoved` (fire when a tag
  enters use inside the DataModel — its first holder in the tree — and when it leaves use — its last
  holder in the tree loses it or leaves; a nil-parented holder counts for neither, and GetAllTags
  lists only tags in use; once per tag, not once per instance — use the per-tag signals for that).
  Entering/leaving the tree fires Added/Removed exactly like Add/RemoveTag would. Duplicate
  `AddTag` is a no-op; `RemoveTag` of a tag never held is a no-op. `GetCollection`, `ItemAdded`,
  `ItemRemoved` are Deprecated in the mirror and are simply absent here (unknown-member, not a
  NOT_IMPLEMENTED stub).
    local cs = game:GetService(""CollectionService"")
    cs:AddTag(part, ""kill"")
    cs:GetInstanceAddedSignal(""kill""):Connect(function(inst) inst:Destroy() end)

```lua
local m = Instance.new(""Model"")
m:SetAttribute(""Team"", ""Red"")
m:SetAttribute(""Score"", 10)
m:SetAttribute(""Spawn"", Vector3.new(1, 2, 3))
m:AddTag(""Enemy"")
m.Parent = workspace
print(m:GetAttribute(""Team"") .. "" "" .. tostring(m:GetAttribute(""Score"")))
print(tostring(m:HasTag(""Enemy"")))  -- true
```

## Value objects

- `IntValue`, `NumberValue`, `StringValue`, `BoolValue`, `ObjectValue`, `Vector3Value`,
  `CFrameValue`, `Color3Value` — all `Instance.new`-creatable; every one `IsA(""ValueBase"")`, the
  common abstract ancestor (`ValueBase` itself is NOT creatable).
- One member, `.Value` (read+write, type-checked; numbers and numeric strings convert as in
  section datatypes): a wrong-type write raises BAD_ARGUMENT and
  leaves the old value AND revision untouched. Defaults: Int/Number 0, String """", Bool false,
  Object nil, Vector3/CFrame their datatype's zero/identity, Color3 black (CoreAI's own default;
  the mirror does not specify one). `.Changed` fires once with the new value on every real change
  (assigning the SAME value again does not fire).
- `IntValue.Value` rounds to the nearest integer, halfway away from zero (`2.5`->3, `-2.5`->-3);
  a value outside -2^63..2^63-1 is BAD_ARGUMENT.
  `StringValue.Value` is capped at 200,000 characters; longer raises ""String too long"".
  `ObjectValue.Value` accepts an Instance or nil.
- `leaderstats` is CONVENTION, not an API: a `Folder` named exactly `""leaderstats""` parented
  under a `Player`, holding named Value children — the standard way to show per-player stats; its
  IntValue/NumberValue/StringValue children show in the player list (section data, with saving).
    local coins = Instance.new(""IntValue"")
    coins.Name = ""Coins""; coins.Value = 0
    local stats = Instance.new(""Folder"")
    stats.Name = ""leaderstats""
    coins.Parent = stats
    stats.Parent = player

<!-- section: parts -->
# Part write rules, Shape, materials, MaterialVariant, Raycast, Touched, Gravity

Booleans accept only true/false; number properties also accept numeric strings; string properties
accept numbers; Enum properties accept the item, its Name or its Value (part.Material = ""Wood""). `Size` is
clamped to [0.001, 2048] per axis; a NaN or infinite Position/Size/CFrame is BAD_ARGUMENT; a
written CFrame is orthonormalized. An unanchored part reads its live physics pose. A wrong type
reads `Part.Anchored expects a boolean, got string | fix: assign a boolean to Part.Anchored`; a read-only
property says it is read only.
`Shape` accepts `Enum.PartType.Ball`, `.Block`, `.Cylinder`, `.Wedge`, `.CornerWedge` — every
one of them materializes its real mesh.
`Material` accepts any of the 45 `Enum.Material` items and all 45 render. Thirty-six of them
use textured PBR surfaces shipped inside the package; the rest are procedural.
`MaterialVariant` (string, """" for none) is how you use YOUR OWN textures instead of the built-in ones:
create a `MaterialVariant` under `MaterialService`, point it at your texture paths, then name it on a part.
  local mv = Instance.new(""MaterialVariant"")
  mv.Name = ""MossyBrick""; mv.BaseMaterial = Enum.Material.Brick
  mv.ColorMap = ""MyGame/Textures/mossy_color""   -- a Resources path in your project
  mv.NormalMap = ""MyGame/Textures/mossy_normal""; mv.StudsPerTile = 8
  mv.Parent = game:GetService(""MaterialService"")
  wall.MaterialVariant = ""MossyBrick""    -- swap on; wall.MaterialVariant = """" swaps back
A map you leave empty is inherited from `BaseMaterial`, so recolouring a surface is three lines.
Editing a live variant repaints every part already wearing it. Naming a variant that does not
exist renders the plain `Material` — that is not an error.
A material id outside the enum resolves to a magenta diagnostic material instead of failing
quietly, so a wrong value is visible at once.
`Color` is an independent Roblox-style tint. A part whose `Color` was never assigned shows the
material's own albedo; assigning `Color` modulates that albedo from then on. `Neon` is the one
material with no palette of its own — its emission IS `Part.Color`, so set `Color` to pick the glow.

## Physics (Raycast, Gravity, Touched)

- `workspace:Raycast(origin, direction[, raycastParams])` -> RaycastResult or nil. `direction`'s
  length is capped at 15,000 studs inclusive; longer, zero-length, or non-finite origin/direction
  raise BAD_ARGUMENT before the engine is ever queried. RaycastResult fields: `Instance` (the hit
  BasePart), `Position`, `Normal` (both Vector3), `Material` (Enum.Material), `Distance` (number);
  the result is immutable userdata, read-only. A ray can also hit Terrain (section terrain).
- `RaycastParams.new()` — fields `FilterType` (Enum.RaycastFilterType: only `Exclude` (default)
  and `Include` exist), `FilterDescendantsInstances` (an array of instances), `IgnoreWater` (bool,
  accepted but a no-op), `CollisionGroup` (string; only `""Default""` is accepted — anything else
  raises BAD_ARGUMENT), `BruteForceAllSlow` (bool, accepted but a no-op). Method
  `:AddToFilter(list)` appends to `FilterDescendantsInstances`. `Exclude` skips the listed
  instances and their descendants; `Include` makes ONLY the listed subtree eligible; an empty list
  means everything is eligible. `:AddToFilter` takes one instance or an array. `ExcludeInstances`
  / `IncludeInstances` are arrays too (exclusion wins; `IncludeInstances = nil` means everything,
  `{}` means nothing). `RespectCanCollide = true` skips CanCollide=false parts.
    local params = RaycastParams.new()
    params.FilterType = Enum.RaycastFilterType.Exclude
    params.FilterDescendantsInstances = {player.Character}
    local hit = workspace:Raycast(origin, direction * 100, params)
    if hit then print(hit.Instance.Name, hit.Distance) end
- `workspace.Gravity` (number, read+write, studs/s^2) — default `196.2`. Must be finite; NaN or
  infinity raise BAD_ARGUMENT and leave the old value in place. Writing needs WorldEdit.
- `BasePart.Touched` / `.TouchEnded` (RbxScriptSignal, fire `(otherPart)`) — created on first read.
  A contact fires Touched on BOTH parts of the pair (`partA.Touched` reports `partB` and vice
  versa); several engine contact points for one collision collapse to a single fire. Setting a
  part's `CFrame` (a teleport) so it ends up overlapping another part does NOT fire Touched for
  that physics step — the suppression lasts exactly one step, then contacts fire normally again.
- `CanCollide = false` works as in Roblox: bodies pass through the part, but it still fires
  Touched/TouchEnded and `workspace:Raycast` still hits it — a coin or trap zone is a
  CanCollide=false part with a Touched handler. Cylinders are hit and touched too. Only parts under
  `workspace` are physical; parts in ReplicatedStorage, ServerStorage or Lighting do nothing.

<!-- section: tweens -->
# TweenService animation

- `TweenService:Create(instance, TweenInfo.new(time[, easingStyle, easingDirection, repeatCount,
  reverses, delayTime]), {Property = goal, ...})` returns a Tween. `:Play()`, `:Pause()`,
  `:Cancel()`; `.PlaybackState` (Enum.PlaybackState); `.Completed` fires once with the ending
  state (`Completed` or `Cancelled` — Pause fires nothing, and a second `:Play()` on the same
  part cancels whatever tween was already running there first). Tweens advance on
  `RunService.Heartbeat` SCALED time, so a paused world (delta 0) freezes them exactly.
  `TweenService:GetValue(alpha, easingStyle, easingDirection)` -> eased number clamped to [0,1].
  `TweenInfo.new()` defaults: time 1, `Enum.EasingStyle.Quad`, `Enum.EasingDirection.Out`,
  repeatCount 0, reverses false, delayTime 0. `SmoothDamp` is a loud stub (NOT_IMPLEMENTED) —
  interpolate manually with `GetValue` over `Heartbeat` instead.
  Create/Play/Pause/Cancel need WorldEdit and write access to the target. Tweenable: number,
  Vector3, CFrame, Color3, UDim2 (parts, `CurrentCamera.CFrame` and `FieldOfView`, Humanoid numbers,
  value objects, Lighting's ClockTime/Brightness/Ambient/OutdoorAmbient, a PointLight's
  Brightness/Color/Range);
  boolean/EnumItem/UDim/Vector2 members raise NOT_IMPLEMENTED; a NaN/inf goal is BAD_ARGUMENT at
  Create. With `reverses` every repeat is a forward and a backward leg; time 0 completes at once.
  A tweened move never fires Touched. A tween that faults is cancelled (Completed(Cancelled)).
  WRONG: keep one tween and `:Play()` it again much later — an actor keeps only its 256 most
  recent finished tweens, and an older one raises INSTANCE_DESTROYED. RIGHT: call
  `TweenService:Create` each time you animate.
    local tw = game:GetService(""TweenService""):Create(part,
        TweenInfo.new(1, Enum.EasingStyle.Quad, Enum.EasingDirection.Out), {Transparency = 1})
    tw.Completed:Connect(function(state) print(state) end)  -- Enum.PlaybackState.Completed
    tw:Play()

<!-- section: messaging -->
# RemoteEvent, RemoteFunction, BindableEvent/Function between mods

- `RemoteEvent`/`UnreliableRemoteEvent` fire one-way; an `UnreliableRemoteEvent` payload over
  1,000 bytes raises PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE, in solo too. `RemoteFunction:InvokeServer/InvokeClient`
  yields for a reply and is bounded to 30 scheduler seconds: a missing or stalled receiver raises
  an error instead of yielding forever (a deliberate deviation from Roblox). Wrap a fallible
  invoke in `pcall`.
- `BindableEvent` (`:Fire(...)`, `.Event`) and `BindableFunction` (`:Invoke(...)`, `.OnInvoke = fn`) work as in
  Roblox between scripts and mods: use them, not workspace attributes, to talk between mods. `Fire` never
  yields; `Invoke` yields until the callback returns, runs it as the mod that assigned it, raises the
  callback's error in the invoker, and waits (no timeout) while no `OnInvoke` is set. `OnInvoke` is write-only.
  Arguments and results are copied by the RemoteEvent rules: tables copied without metatable, non-string keys
  become strings, functions arrive as nil; a mixed array/dictionary table or a cycle raises BAD_ARGUMENT, more
  than 64 KiB PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE.

<!-- section: data -->
# DataStoreService, leaderstats

`DataStoreService` keeps data between sessions, Roblox's way. A store is shared by every mod of the world
(same name and scope = same store); name collisions are yours to avoid. Needs the `Data` capability (part of
`All`; a header that lists its tiers adds `Data`).

```lua
local DataStoreService = game:GetService(""DataStoreService"")
local store = DataStoreService:GetDataStore(""PlayerData"")   -- scope defaults to ""global""
local Players = game:GetService(""Players"")

local coinsOf = {}                               -- userId -> coins: the session copy you save from

Players.PlayerAdded:Connect(function(player)
    local leaderstats = Instance.new(""Folder"")
    leaderstats.Name = ""leaderstats""          -- exact lowercase name, directly under the Player
    leaderstats.Parent = player
    local coins = Instance.new(""IntValue"")
    coins.Name = ""Coins""                      -- column header of the player list
    coins.Parent = leaderstats
    local ok, saved = pcall(function() return store:GetAsync(""Player_"" .. player.UserId) end)
    if ok and saved then coins.Value = saved.coins end
    coinsOf[player.UserId] = coins.Value
    coins.Changed:Connect(function(value) coinsOf[player.UserId] = value end)
end)

local function save(userId)
    local coins = coinsOf[userId]
    if coins == nil then return end
    pcall(function()
        store:UpdateAsync(""Player_"" .. userId, function(old)
            return { coins = coins }                  -- return nil to cancel the write
        end)
    end)
end
-- In PlayerRemoving the Player is already gone: read only player.UserId, save from your table.
Players.PlayerRemoving:Connect(function(player) save(player.UserId) coinsOf[player.UserId] = nil end)
task.spawn(function() while true do task.wait(60) for userId in pairs(coinsOf) do save(userId) end end end)
```

- `GetAsync(key)` -> value, keyInfo (nil, nil for a new key). `SetAsync(key, value, userIds?)` -> version
  string. `UpdateAsync(key, function(old, keyInfo) return new, userIds?, metadata? end)` -> new, keyInfo;
  the function must not yield and should only compute (return nil = no write). `IncrementAsync(key, delta=1)`
  integers only. `RemoveAsync(key)` -> old value, keyInfo. keyInfo: `Version`, `CreatedTime`, `UpdatedTime`,
  `GetUserIds()`, `GetMetadata()`.
- Every one of them YIELDS: call them from the mod's chunk, `task.spawn`, or a signal handler, and wrap them
  in `pcall`: they fail like Roblox's (the code is inside the message). An execute_lua chunk cannot wait:
  save from a mod.
- Values: booleans, numbers, strings and tables that are arrays OR string-keyed dictionaries of those. No
  Instances, Vector3/CFrame/Color3, functions or mixed tables (`104: Can't store ...`): store
  `{ x = v.X, y = v.Y, z = v.Z }` instead. Keys, names and scopes are 1-50 characters (`101`/`102`); a value
  is at most 4,194,304 characters of JSON (`105`). One string you build is at most 1,000,000 characters
  (`string.rep`, `table.concat`), so a value that large is a table of strings.
- Pacing per world: 100 reads, 100 writes and 100 removes a minute in a solo world (60 + 40 per player).
  Over budget a request waits in a queue of 30; a full queue fails at once with
  `30x: SetAsync request dropped. Request was throttled but queue was full.` Retry after a `task.wait`, keep
  the data in a table, save on leave and every minute, and check
  `DataStoreService:GetRequestBudgetForRequestType(Enum.DataStoreRequestType.SetIncrementAsync)`.
- A world keeps at most 64 MiB (8 MiB on WebGL) and 16,384 keys; past that writes fail with
  BUDGET_EXCEEDED. `501` means a stored file was unreadable (set aside, the store starts empty); `502` means the
  game's storage refused a write: retry later.
- Data is NOT part of the world: a world save or load does not carry it and `/undo` does not change it.
  A write is kept in memory at once and reaches disk within about a second and when the world closes.
- Not built yet (NOT_IMPLEMENTED, MVP16): `GetOrderedDataStore` (keep a top-10 table in one key instead),
  `ListKeysAsync`, the version calls, and the options objects (`DataStoreSetOptions`, ...): leave the options
  argument out.

## leaderstats
- A `Folder` named exactly `leaderstats` directly under a Player, with `IntValue`, `NumberValue` or
  `StringValue` children, shows the player list at the top right: one column per value (its Name, in creation
  order, at most 4), one row per player, live values. Nothing is drawn while no player has a stat.
- `game:GetService(""StarterGui""):SetCoreGuiEnabled(Enum.CoreGuiType.PlayerList, false)` hides it (true shows).
- It draws under your own UI: put your HUD anywhere, the list never covers it.

<!-- section: terrain -->
# Block worlds: Terrain cells, FillBlock, FillRegion, WriteVoxels

`workspace.Terrain` is a grid of 4 x 4 x 4-stud cells, each holding one `Enum.Material` and an
occupancy 0..1. CoreAI draws every non-empty cell as a full cube, so it IS a block (voxel) world: use it
for ground, caves, walls and anything built from many blocks. One Part per block does not scale (every
Part is an instance; a world allows a few thousand), a Terrain holds millions of cells. In a block world
use Parts for props, doors, moving things and anything that needs Color or Transparency.
`workspace.Terrain` exists in every world and cannot be created, destroyed or moved.

- Cells: cell `(x, y, z)` spans studs `x*4 .. x*4+4` (same on y, z). `terrain:WorldToCell(pos)` -> Vector3
  of whole cell numbers; `terrain:CellCenterToWorld(x, y, z)` and `terrain:CellCornerToWorld(x, y, z)` ->
  Vector3 in studs.
- `terrain:FillBlock(cframe, size, material)` fills every cell whose CENTRE is inside the box (studs, may
  be rotated). One block: `FillBlock(CFrame.new(terrain:CellCenterToWorld(x, y, z)), Vector3.new(4, 4, 4), m)`.
  `Enum.Material.Air` breaks blocks. A box centred on a grid corner fills the cells it half-covers.
- `Region3.new(min, max)` (studs; `.CFrame`, `.Size`, `:ExpandToGrid(4)`). Region calls need corners on
  multiples of 4 (`region:ExpandToGrid(4)` fixes that), resolution exactly `4`, and at most 4,194,304 cells
  per call (256 x 64 x 256 cells); otherwise BAD_ARGUMENT.
- `terrain:FillRegion(region, 4, material)`, `terrain:ReplaceMaterial(region, 4, from, to)` (`from = Air`
  fills empty cells, `to = Air` clears), `terrain:Clear()`, `terrain:CountCells()`.
- `terrain:ReadVoxels(region, 4)` -> `materials, occupancies`, 3D arrays `t[x][y][z]` (1-based) with a
  `Size` field (Vector3, in cells). `terrain:WriteVoxels(region, 4, materials, occupancies)` writes arrays of
  exactly that size. For generating a whole area, build the arrays and make ONE WriteVoxels call per
  region: per cell it is far cheaper than a FillBlock per cell, which runs the whole call path each time.
- Materials: only the Terrain materials — `Air, Water, Grass, LeafyGrass, Ground, Mud, Rock, Slate,
  Basalt, CrackedLava, Sand, Sandstone, Snow, Ice, Glacier, Salt, Limestone, Brick, Cobblestone,
  Concrete, Pavement, Asphalt, WoodPlanks`. Plastic, Neon, Wood, Glass and other Part materials raise
  BAD_ARGUMENT. Water draws as transparent blocks you can walk through (no swimming) and rays pass through
  it. Each block draws as a block-game tile: Grass is a grass block (earth sides), LeafyGrass is leaves (use it
  for tree crowns), Mud is tree bark (use it for trunks; there is no log material), WoodPlanks is planks, Ground
  is dirt, Rock is stone, Cobblestone is cobbles; corners where blocks meet are shaded.
- `workspace:Raycast` on a block answers `Instance == workspace.Terrain`, the cell's `Material` and the
  face `Normal` (an axis vector): add `Normal * 2` to `Position` and call `WorldToCell` to get the cell to
  place into, subtract it to get the cell to break. Filter Terrain in or out with RaycastParams like any
  instance. Rays hit Terrain in every world, also on a server or a headless tool without a physics engine. `workspace.Terrain:IsA(""BasePart"")` is false in CoreAI, so check `hit.Instance == workspace.Terrain`.
- Writes need WorldEdit. The world has bounds and a cell budget: past them a write raises BAD_ARGUMENT or
  BUDGET_EXCEEDED and changes nothing.
- A generator that rebuilds cells from a seed sets `workspace:SetAttribute(""CoreAITerrainGenerating"", true)`
  around its writes (false after): a world kept between starts then records only real edits.
- Not implemented (NOT_IMPLEMENTED): FillBall, FillCylinder, FillWedge, ReadVoxelChannels,
  WriteVoxelChannels, CopyRegion, PasteRegion, GetMaterialColor/SetMaterialColor, the Water* properties.

```lua
local terrain = workspace.Terrain

-- Ground: a 64 x 4 x 64-cell grass slab whose top face is at y = 16 studs.
terrain:FillBlock(CFrame.new(128, 8, 128), Vector3.new(256, 16, 256), Enum.Material.Grass)

-- Place and break single blocks by cell.
local function place(x, y, z, material)
	terrain:FillBlock(CFrame.new(terrain:CellCenterToWorld(x, y, z)), Vector3.new(4, 4, 4), material)
end
place(10, 4, 10, Enum.Material.Brick)
place(10, 3, 10, Enum.Material.Air)

-- Generate an area with one WriteVoxels call.
local region = Region3.new(Vector3.new(64, 16, 0), Vector3.new(128, 32, 64))
local materials, occupancies = terrain:ReadVoxels(region, 4)
local size = materials.Size
for x = 1, size.X do
	for y = 1, size.Y do
		for z = 1, size.Z do
			local solid = y <= 2 and (x + z) % 3 == 0
			materials[x][y][z] = solid and Enum.Material.Rock or Enum.Material.Air
			occupancies[x][y][z] = solid and 1 or 0
		end
	end
end
terrain:WriteVoxels(region, 4, materials, occupancies)

-- The block under a point, and the cell to place a block on top of it.
local hit = workspace:Raycast(Vector3.new(42, 100, 42), Vector3.new(0, -200, 0))
if hit and hit.Instance == terrain then
	local above = terrain:WorldToCell(hit.Position + hit.Normal * 2)
	place(above.X, above.Y, above.Z, Enum.Material.Cobblestone)
end
```

<!-- section: building -->
# Build at the crosshair in cells (sizes); towers of Parts; SelectionBox outlines; what a save keeps, what /undo takes back

Building at the crosshair (block worlds). When the player says ""here"", ""this spot"" or ""where I look"", build
at the crosshair, in cells. Block games publish it as workspace attributes: `CrosshairPlaceCell` (Vector3 of
whole cell numbers: the empty cell in front of the face the player looks at; build up from it),
`CrosshairCell` (the block looked at), `CrosshairNormal` (that face's normal) and `CrosshairHasTarget` (false
for the sky or beyond reach). If the chat turn names a target cell, use that. If there is no target, don't
guess: fail with ""point the crosshair at a block first"". Sizes are in cells of 4 studs: a watchtower is about
3 x 8 x 3 with a 5 x 5 platform, a small house 5 x 4 x 7. Fill whole boxes with `FillRegion` from
`CellCornerToWorld(x0, y0, z0)` to `CellCornerToWorld(x1 + 1, y1 + 1, z1 + 1)`, hollow them with
`Enum.Material.Air`, use Terrain materials only, and keep every write inside the box you describe, so `/undo`
takes it all back.
```lua
local terrain = workspace.Terrain
local base = workspace:GetAttribute('CrosshairPlaceCell')
if typeof(base) ~= 'Vector3' then
  error('point the crosshair at a block first')
end
local function fill(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, material)
  local min = terrain:CellCornerToWorld(x0, y0, z0)
  local max = terrain:CellCornerToWorld(x1 + 1, y1 + 1, z1 + 1)
  terrain:FillRegion(Region3.new(min, max), 4, material)
end
local x, y, z = base.X, base.Y, base.Z
fill(x - 1, y, z - 1, x + 1, y + 7, z + 1, Enum.Material.Cobblestone)
fill(x, y + 1, z, x, y + 7, z, Enum.Material.Air)
fill(x - 2, y + 8, z - 2, x + 2, y + 8, z + 2, Enum.Material.WoodPlanks)
for _, corner in ipairs({ { -2, -2 }, { 2, -2 }, { -2, 2 }, { 2, 2 } }) do
  fill(x + corner[1], y + 9, z + corner[2], x + corner[1], y + 9, z + corner[2], Enum.Material.WoodPlanks)
end
```

Outline what the player targets with a `SelectionBox` (works as in Roblox), not with twelve Parts: set
`Adornee` (a Part, or a Model: the box around all its parts), `Color3`, `LineThickness` (studs, default 0.15),
`Transparency`, `SurfaceColor3`/`SurfaceTransparency` (faces; default 1 = none) and `Visible`, and parent it
to `workspace` (or a PlayerGui). It follows the adornee, is never hit by a ray and needs no `Ui` capability.
To outline a Terrain cell, adorn one anchored `Transparency = 1`, `CanCollide = false` Part moved to the cell,
and leave that Part out of your own rays (`RaycastParams`), because a ray still hits a non-colliding Part.

Build a colored tower of parts:
```lua
for i = 1, 5 do
  local p = Instance.new(""Part"")
  p.Name = ""Block"" .. i
  p.Size = Vector3.new(4, 1, 4)
  p.Position = Vector3.new(0, i * 1.2, 0)
  p.Color = Color3.fromRGB(40 * i, 120, 255 - 30 * i)
  p.Anchored = true
  p.Parent = workspace
end
```

Saving and `/undo`. A saved world keeps every world-owned instance with its properties, Terrain cells, mod
sources, the Lighting day cycle, StarterPlayer and StarterGui settings, and GUI under `StarterGui` (copied into
each player's PlayerGui on spawn). It does not keep what a mod creates (the mod rebuilds it when it starts), the
PlayerGui copies or characters. The player's `/undo` takes back everything one of your calls changed, settings
alone included (""make it night""), so make one request in one call; it refuses once a player, a mod or another
agent changed the world since, and you cannot call it: tell the player to type `/undo`.

<!-- section: characters -->
# Humanoid, Players, Player.Character, respawn

- `Instance.new(""Humanoid"")` — health, movement parameters and a small state machine; NOT a full
  character rig (no body parts, no auto-created Model/Script). Defaults: `MaxHealth` 100, `Health`
  100, `WalkSpeed` 16, `JumpPower` 50, `JumpHeight` 7.2, `UseJumpPower` true.
- `Health` is clamped to `[0, MaxHealth]`; lowering `MaxHealth` clamps `Health` down with it.
  `:TakeDamage(amount)` lowers Health (a negative amount heals). Reaching 0 fires `Died` exactly
  once; a dead Humanoid does not resurrect from further Health writes and stays `IsDead == true`.
  There is NO passive health regeneration in the class itself (the mirror puts that in a Script
  inserted into the character; nothing heals a CoreAI Humanoid on its own).
  `Died` fires only inside `workspace` (a Humanoid at 0 outside it dies on its first Heartbeat
  inside). `MaxHealth = math.huge` is allowed, but then `Health == math.huge` is false — compare
  with `MaxHealth`; `TakeDamage(math.huge)` kills; NaN is BAD_ARGUMENT. `JumpPower` is clamped to
  [0, 1000]. TakeDamage/MoveTo/ChangeState need WorldEdit and write access to that Humanoid, so a
  mod cannot damage another actor's character (route PvP damage through server code). Writes to
  Health, MaxHealth, WalkSpeed, JumpPower, JumpHeight, UseJumpPower and DisplayName fire Changed.
- `:MoveTo(position)` walks toward `position` and fires `MoveToFinished(reached: bool)` — `true`
  on arrival (within ~1 stud on the ground plane; height is ignored), `false` after an
  8-SCALED-second timeout (a paused world never times it out), and `false` when a script or a
  tween moves the HumanoidRootPart (CFrame/Position/Orientation/Rotation, or PivotTo).
  `:MoveTo(position, part)` (follow a moving part) is refused with BAD_ARGUMENT, not silently
  downgraded. Setting `Jump = true` requests one jump (fires `Jumping(true)`) using
  `JumpPower`/`JumpHeight`/`UseJumpPower`; jumping and moving both do nothing while dead.
- `:Move(direction, relativeToCamera = false)` walks along a direction until called again;
  `Vector3.new(0, 0, -1)` with `true` walks where the camera looks (flattened to the ground). A
  non-zero Move ends a MoveTo in progress (MoveToFinished(false)); `Move(Vector3.zero)` stops.
  The bundled `coreai_player_controls` mod already drives the local character with WASD/arrows,
  the gamepad left stick and Space; turn it off with
  `game:GetService('StarterPlayer').DevComputerMovementMode = Enum.DevComputerMovementMode.Scriptable`
  before writing your own controls.
- `:GetState()` -> an `Enum.HumanoidStateType` item; `StateChanged(old, new)` fires on transitions
  the character motor reports (e.g. Freefall/Landed/Running from ground contact). `:ChangeState`
  may force `Enum.HumanoidStateType.Jumping`, `Flying` (free flight: no gravity, Move keeps its Y
  part; CoreAI-specific) and `Freefall` (ends a flight) — any other state raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED. `Sit`
  is a loud stub (`h.Sit = true` raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED).
- `MoveDirection` (read-only Vector3) mirrors the character controller's current motion.
- `humanoid:Clone()` keeps Health, MaxHealth, WalkSpeed, JumpPower, JumpHeight, UseJumpPower and
  DisplayName; a clone of a dead Humanoid (Health 0) dies on its first Heartbeat in `workspace`.

## Players & characters

- `game:GetService(""Players"")` — `:GetPlayers()` -> array of connected Players; `:GetPlayerByUserId(id)`
  and `:GetPlayerFromCharacter(model)` -> Player or nil (never error on a miss; only live Players
  under Players count). `.PlayerAdded(player)`
  and `.PlayerRemoving(player, reason)` fire on connect/disconnect. `Players.LocalPlayer` is the Player
  of the actor a mod was loaded for (the bundled control script runs that way); a mod the host loads is
  a server script and reads nil: reach players there with `GetPlayers()` and `PlayerAdded`.
- `Players.CharacterAutoLoads` (bool, default true) — when true, a joining actor gets a character
  automatically. Set it from HOST composition, not from a script: a joining actor can already be
  connected (and its character already queued to spawn) before any mod chunk has run, so a script
  cannot reliably race the join to flip this in time.
- `Players.RespawnTime` (number, studs-per-second-free — plain seconds, default 5) — how long after
  a character's `Humanoid.Died` fires before a fresh one autoloads, when `CharacterAutoLoads` is
  still true when the timer elapses.
- `player.Character` — the Model driven for this player, or nil until loaded. Assigning it directly
  does NOT fire the signals below (only `LoadCharacterAsync` does), though it fires `Changed`.
- `player.CharacterAdded(character)` / `player.CharacterRemoving(character)` fire on load/replace.
  Replacing an existing character fires `CharacterRemoving` for the outgoing one BEFORE
  `CharacterAdded` for its replacement.
- `player:LoadCharacterAsync()` — builds (or rebuilds) the character: a Model named after the player,
  parented to `workspace`, holding a `Humanoid` and the R6 body (section `rig`;
  `humanoid.RootPart` is its `HumanoidRootPart`). YIELDS until the deferred CharacterAdded/CharacterRemoving handlers have run, so code after
  the call sees the finished character and its signals already delivered. `player:LoadCharacter()` is
  a deprecated non-yielding alias (logs once per mod) with the same effect.
- `player:DistanceFromCharacter(point)` -> studs from the character's root part to `point`, or `0`
  when the player has no character (never errors on a characterless player).
- A character Model has `Archivable = false`, so `character:Clone()` returns nil — set
  `character.Archivable = true` first to copy it.
- `player:Kick([message])` removes the player; the message (a string, cut to 1,024 UTF-8 bytes) is
  shown to that player's client. A number is sent as its tostring text; any other non-string is
  BAD_ARGUMENT.
    local Players = game:GetService(""Players"")
    Players.PlayerAdded:Connect(function(player)
      player.CharacterAdded:Connect(function(character)
        character.Humanoid.Died:Connect(function() print(player.Name .. "" died"") end)
      end)
    end)

<!-- section: rig -->
# R6 body parts, built-in animation

- A player's character is the classic R6 body, real Parts in its Model: `HumanoidRootPart` (the
  invisible 2x2x1 box the motor moves; `character.PrimaryPart`), `Torso`, `Head`, `Left Arm`,
  `Right Arm`, `Left Leg`, `Right Leg`, and the `Humanoid`. Reach them as in Roblox
  (`character.Head`, `character:FindFirstChild(""Left Arm"")`) and recolour them like any Part.
- ANY Model in `workspace` with a `Humanoid` and those part names animates by itself (idle, walk with
  a stride that follows the speed, jump, fall, land, fly); no Animate script. For an NPC: an
  UNANCHORED `HumanoidRootPart`, limbs unanchored with `CanCollide = false`, then walk it with
  `Humanoid:MoveTo`/`:Move`. Never write limb CFrames each frame: the rig owns them. An anchored root
  stands idle, an anchored limb is left where it is, other part names are left alone.
- The Torso sits 2 studs above the root, the Head 3.5 (the root's box rests on the floor); the camera looks at the head.
- A walking character turns to face where it goes; `humanoid.AutoRotate = false` leaves the heading to you.
- `humanoid.StateChanged:Connect(function(old, new) if new == Enum.HumanoidStateType.Freefall ...)`
  is the way to react to a jump or a fall; do not poll GetState.
- Not built yet (NOT_IMPLEMENTED): `Motor6D`/`Weld` joints, `Animator`, `Animation`,
  `Humanoid:LoadAnimation` and its tracks (planned with MVP17), `HipHeight`, `RigType`. R15 and
  accessories are not planned.

<!-- section: input -->
# UserInputService, gameProcessed, ClickDetector, a keyboard game loop

Roblox-1:1 keyboard/mouse. Get it with `game:GetService(""UserInputService"")`, or use the global
`UserInputService` (same instance). Reading input is Read-tier — no WorldEdit needed.

- Events (fire `(input, gameProcessedEvent)`): `InputBegan`, `InputEnded`, `InputChanged`.
  `input` is an InputObject with `.KeyCode` (Enum.KeyCode), `.UserInputType` (Enum.UserInputType),
  `.UserInputState` (Enum.UserInputState), `.Position` (Vector3), `.Delta` (Vector3).
  `:Connect(fn)` and `:Once(fn)` return an RBXScriptConnection with `.Connected` and
  `:Disconnect()`; `:Wait()` yields until the next fire and returns its arguments.
- Polls: `IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode)` -> bool; `GetKeysPressed()` -> array of InputObject;
  `GetMouseLocation()` -> Vector2 (top-left origin).
- `MouseBehavior` (read+write, Enum.MouseBehavior: Default/LockCenter/LockCurrentPosition) locks
  the host cursor (LockCurrentPosition recentres it). `GetMouseDelta()` -> Vector2 pixels moved last
  frame, zero unless the pointer is locked, scaled by `MouseDeltaSensitivity` (read+write, 0..10,
  default 1). The mouse wheel fires `InputChanged` with UserInputType MouseWheel and the direction in
  `input.Position.Z` (+1 forward, -1 back). The gamepad sticks fire `InputChanged` with KeyCode
  Thumbstick1/Thumbstick2 and the stick in `input.Position` (x, y in -1..1, y up).
- `TextBoxFocused(textBox)` / `TextBoxFocusReleased(textBox)` fire when a GUI TextBox takes / returns the
  keyboard, and `GetFocusedTextBox()` returns it (section gui). CoreAI's own chat and Hub fields are no
  TextBox: for them the argument is nil and `GetFocusedTextBox()` stays nil.
- `gameProcessedEvent` (the second argument of InputBegan/InputEnded/InputChanged) is `true` while
  CoreAI UI (the chat, the Hub) or your own UI (a TextBox, a UXML field) has text focus, and for mouse
  input while the pointer is over a button, a TextBox, an `Active` GUI object or a UXML element:
  start every handler with `if gameProcessed then return end`.
- `game:GetService('StarterPlayer')`: `DevComputerMovementMode` (Enum.DevComputerMovementMode,
  default UserChoice), `CameraMode` (Enum.CameraMode), `CameraMaxZoomDistance` (400),
  `CameraMinZoomDistance` (0.5); writes need WorldEdit. Each Player copies the three camera settings
  when it joins: `player.CameraMode`, `player.CameraMaxZoomDistance`, `player.CameraMinZoomDistance`
  (read+write) are what the camera uses.
- `ClickDetector` — parent one under a Part to make that 3D part clickable:
  `local cd = Instance.new(""ClickDetector""); cd.Parent = part; cd.MouseClick:Connect(function() ... end)`.
  MouseClick fires `(playerWhoClicked)` when the user clicks THAT part with the mouse and the
  player's character is within `cd.MaxActivationDistance` studs of it (default 32; measured from
  the camera when there is no character). A detector under a Model or Folder covers all of its
  parts; the deepest detector above the clicked part wins. Only the part under the cursor fires.

Drive a part with the keyboard and follow it with the camera (a tiny mini-game loop):
```lua
local UIS = game:GetService(""UserInputService"")
local car = Instance.new(""Part"")
car.Size = Vector3.new(4, 1, 8)
car.Position = Vector3.new(0, 1, 0)
car.Anchored = true
car.Parent = workspace
workspace.CurrentCamera.CameraSubject = car             -- the default camera orbits the part

-- The game loop is RunService.Heartbeat — the Roblox per-frame hook. dt is the frame delta in
-- seconds; multiply speeds by dt so motion is smooth and frame-rate independent (studs per second).
local RunService = game:GetService(""RunService"")
local SPEED = 12                                         -- studs per second
RunService.Heartbeat:Connect(function(dt)
  local move = Vector3.zero
  if UIS:IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode.W) then move = move + Vector3.new(0, 0, -1) end
  if UIS:IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode.S) then move = move + Vector3.new(0, 0, 1) end
  if UIS:IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode.A) then move = move + Vector3.new(-1, 0, 0) end
  if UIS:IsKeyDown(Enum.KeyCode.D) then move = move + Vector3.new(1, 0, 0) end
  car.Position = car.Position + move * SPEED * dt
end)

UIS.InputBegan:Connect(function(input, gameProcessed)
  if gameProcessed then return end                       -- typing in chat or a UI field
  if input.KeyCode == Enum.KeyCode.Space then
    car.Color = Color3.fromRGB(255, 80, 80)               -- flash on the jump key
  end
end)
```

<!-- section: camera -->
# CurrentCamera, screen rays, the default camera, framing a game

`workspace.CurrentCamera` is the Camera instance. Members: `CFrame` (read+write — the camera
pose in studs, over the engine camera rig), `CameraType` (Enum.CameraType, read+write),
`CameraSubject` (a Humanoid or BasePart, read+write; reads the local character's Humanoid until you
assign one), `FieldOfView` (vertical degrees, read+write, default 70, clamped 1..120),
`ViewportSize` (Vector2 pixels, read-only). Queries: `cam:ViewportPointToRay(x, y, depth = 0)` and
`cam:ScreenPointToRay(x, y, depth = 0)` -> a unit `Ray` through a pixel (top-left origin);
`cam:WorldToViewportPoint(v3)` / `cam:WorldToScreenPoint(v3)` -> `Vector3(x, y, depth), onScreen`.
No top bar, so the screen and viewport variants agree. `Ray.new(origin, direction)` has `Origin`,
`Direction`, `Unit`, `:ClosestPoint(p)`, `:Distance(p)`; cast it with
`workspace:Raycast(ray.Origin, ray.Direction * 500)`. Reads are ungated; writes need WorldEdit.
Convenience globals (WorldEdit): `camera_set_cframe(cf)` sets the pose and
`camera_follow(instance_or_nil)` attaches/clears the follow subject — same effect as writing
`CurrentCamera.CFrame` / `CurrentCamera.CameraSubject`.
Assigning `workspace.CurrentCamera` raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED, and so do `FieldOfViewMode`, `NearPlaneZ` and `Focus`.

The DEFAULT CAMERA runs while `CameraType` is `Custom` (the default) and there is a subject: it
orbits the subject's head behind it, right-drag (or a locked pointer, or the right stick) turns it,
the wheel zooms from 12.5 studs out to the player's `CameraMaxZoomDistance` and in to first person
(pointer locked to the centre), and a wall pulls it in. `player.CameraMode =
Enum.CameraMode.LockFirstPerson` forces first person. In `Custom` your own `CFrame` writes are
overwritten next frame — set `CameraType = Enum.CameraType.Scriptable` first, exactly as in Roblox.
Close to first person the default camera hides the local character on this machine only, as Roblox does:
it sets `LocalTransparencyModifier` (a local, never replicated BasePart property, drawn as
`1 - (1 - Transparency) * (1 - LocalTransparencyModifier)`) on that character's parts; other characters stay drawn.

## Camera framing & controls — READ THIS before making a playable mod

With `Scriptable`, `CameraSubject` only FOLLOWS a part's POSITION at a fixed offset; it keeps
whatever direction the camera already faces. For any game where the player expects
""press D = move right on screen"", take manual control of the angle:
`CurrentCamera.CameraType = Enum.CameraType.Scriptable` then `CurrentCamera.CFrame =
CFrame.lookAt(eye, target)`.

CRITICAL rule that trips people up (it behaves the SAME in real Roblox — it is not an engine bug):
the camera's orientation decides which world axis shows up on screen-left vs screen-right. If your
player moves along world +X but your camera looks in a direction where +X is on its LEFT, then D
(which you coded as +X) visibly moves the car LEFT and the controls feel reversed. Two safe fixes:
- **Frame the camera so +X is on screen-right.** A camera placed on the -Z side looking toward +Z
  (e.g. `CFrame.lookAt(Vector3.new(0, 9, 14), Vector3.new(0, 2, -18))`) keeps world +X on the right,
  so `A = -X = left`, `D = +X = right` feel correct. Verify by moving the player +X and checking it
  goes right on screen.
- **Or derive movement from the camera:** move along `CurrentCamera.CFrame.RightVector` (right) and
  `-CurrentCamera.CFrame.LookVector` is forward — then movement always matches the view no matter how
  the camera is angled.

<!-- section: gui -->
# Roblox GUI: ScreenGui, Frame, TextLabel, TextButton, PlayerGui

Two entrances draw on one screen layer: the Roblox GUI classes below and CoreAI's UXML screens (section
uxml). Both need the `Ui` capability and a mod (one-off `execute_lua` chunks cannot own UI), and they share
the budgets and `UI_*` error codes listed in section uxml. A ScreenGui's `DisplayOrder` and a screen's
`SortOrder` are one scale: higher draws on top.

Roblox GUI works as in Roblox: `ScreenGui`, `Frame`, `TextLabel`, `TextButton`, `TextBox`, `ImageLabel`,
`ImageButton`, `ScrollingFrame`, `UICorner`, `UIPadding`, `UIStroke`, `UIScale`, `UIListLayout`,
`UIGridLayout`, `UIAspectRatioConstraint`, `UISizeConstraint` and `StarterGui`, with Roblox's properties,
defaults, layout and signals; `AbsolutePosition`/`AbsoluteSize` are what is drawn, and TweenService tweens
their UDim2, Color3 and number properties. Where CoreAI differs:
- The PlayerGui drawn is this machine's player's. A mod loaded as that player's actor reaches it as
  `Players.LocalPlayer.PlayerGui`; a mod the host loads (LocalPlayer nil, section characters) uses
  `Players:GetPlayers()` / `PlayerAdded`, or puts its ScreenGui in `StarterGui`.
- `StarterGui` is copied into PlayerGui when the character spawns (`ResetOnSpawn = false`: copied once and
  kept). A child added after the spawn is copied on the next frame; a respawn replaces only the copies, so a
  ScreenGui you parented into PlayerGui yourself stays; destroying an original removes its copies; scripts
  are never copied. Of the core-GUI switches (`SetCoreGuiEnabled`) only `PlayerList` draws something (the
  leaderstats list, section data); the others are recorded, and there is no top bar (`IgnoreGuiInset`
  changes nothing).
- Every font draws in the host font, bold or italic by the font's name. `TextScaled` estimates the size and
  `AutomaticSize` fits children, not text (text is not measured; `TextBounds`/`TextFits` raise
  NOT_IMPLEMENTED). `RichText` draws `<b> <i> <u> <s>`, not Roblox's `<font ...>` or `<stroke>`.
- `Image` must be a URL or path the host can load: `rbxassetid://` ids are never downloaded and draw
  nothing. Nine-slice, `UIGradient`, `HoverImage`/`PressedImage` and `ZIndexBehavior.Global` (drawn as
  Sibling) are not drawn as in Roblox.
- A `ScrollingFrame` scrolls as in Roblox: the wheel, a touch drag and its bar move the canvas within
  `CanvasSize` (for a list set `CanvasSize = UDim2.new()` and `AutomaticCanvasSize = Enum.AutomaticSize.Y`),
  and `CanvasPosition` follows (its signal fires; the player's scrolling costs no write budget, a script's
  write does). `ScrollingEnabled = false` and `ScrollingDirection` stop the player, never a script.
  `CanvasPosition` stays within 0 and `AbsoluteCanvasSize - AbsoluteWindowSize`. The bar is flat, in
  `ScrollBarImageColor3`/`ScrollBarImageTransparency` (`ScrollBarThickness`, the two insets and
  `VerticalScrollBarPosition` work); `ElasticBehavior` and the bar images raise NOT_IMPLEMENTED.
- `InputBegan/InputChanged/InputEnded`, `MouseButton1Down/Up`, the `MouseButton2*` and Touch signals and
  `TweenPosition/TweenSize` raise NOT_IMPLEMENTED (planned MVP15); `BillboardGui`, `SurfaceGui`,
  `ViewportFrame` and `CanvasGroup` are backlog.
- A GUI object you listen to `MouseEnter`/`MouseLeave` on takes the pointer, so input over it arrives
  `gameProcessed` as over an `Active` one.
- A ScreenGui counts as a screen, each GUI object as an element, a property write of a shown object as an
  element write.

<!-- section: uxml -->
# UXML/USS screens (UxmlService), UI error codes and budgets, a hotbar HUD

UxmlService (CoreAI-only; Roblox has no markup): interfaces are UXML + USS **text** handed to
`game:GetService(""UxmlService"")` while the game runs.

- `ui:CreateScreen(id, uxml, uss?, {SortOrder = n, Visible = b}?)` -> UiScreen. Calling it again with the
  same id REPLACES that screen (values of named elements survive) — safe to re-run on every reload.
  `ui:GetScreen(id)`, `ui:GetScreens()`: your own screens only (ids are private to your mod).
- UiScreen: `Visible`, `SortOrder` (writable), `Show()`, `Hide()`, `SetUxml(text)`, `SetUss(text)` (both
  return the reports), `Diagnostics`, `Query(sel)`, `QueryAll(sel)`, `GetElement(name)`, `Root`,
  `CreateElement(kind, parent?, name?)`, `Destroy()`, `Destroying`.
- UiElement: `Text`, `Value` (Toggle boolean; Slider/ProgressBar/ListView number; TextField text),
  `Visible`, `Enabled`, `ZOrder` (all writable); `Name`, `Kind`, `Parent`, `IsAlive`;
  `AddClass/RemoveClass/ToggleClass/HasClass/GetClasses`; `SetStyle(prop, value)` -> bool,
  `ClearStyle(prop)`, `GetStyle(prop)`; `SetAttribute(name, value)`; `SetItems({strings})` (ListView);
  `Focus()`, `Blur()`, `Query(sel)`, `CreateChild(kind, name?)`, `Remove()`.
- Signals (ordinary `:Connect`, deferred, dropped on unload): `Clicked()` (buttons also on Enter/gamepad),
  `ValueChanged(newValue)`, `Submitted(text)` (Enter in a single-line TextField).
  `ui.TextInputFocused`, `ui.PointerOverUi`, `ui.TextInputFocusChanged(focused)`.
- Tokens: `ui:SetToken(""--cai-color-accent"", ""rgb(255, 180, 0)"")` restyles YOUR screens only;
  `ui:GetToken(name)`. Theme tokens: `--cai-color-bg/-surface/-surface-raised/-border/-text/-text-muted/
  -accent/-accent-hover/-on-accent`, `--cai-space-1..4`, `--cai-radius`, `--cai-font-size-small/-body/-h1`.
  Theme classes: `cai-panel cai-row cai-column cai-h1 cai-text cai-small cai-muted cai-btn
  cai-btn-primary is-hidden is-selected is-disabled`.
- Elements: `VisualElement Label Button Toggle Slider TextField ProgressBar Image ScrollView ListView`
  (an unknown one becomes a reported placeholder; templates cannot be registered from Lua yet). Write
  `ui:` prefixes with `<ui:UXML xmlns:ui=""UnityEngine.UIElements"">` (or none). Common attributes:
  `name class style tooltip picking-mode focusable enabled`. Give decorative overlays (a crosshair)
  `picking-mode=""Ignore""`, or the pointer counts as ""over UI"".
- USS subset: selectors type, `.class`, `#name`, descendant, `>` child, `:hover :active :focus :disabled
  :checked :root`; `var(--x)`; properties for flex layout (`display position flex-* align-* justify-content
  left top right bottom width height min-/max-* overflow`), box (`margin padding border-width`), border
  (`border-color border-radius`), text (`color font-size -unity-font-style -unity-text-align white-space
  letter-spacing -unity-text-outline text-shadow`), `background-color background-image opacity visibility
  translate scale rotate transform-origin transition`. Lengths `px`/`%`. NOT supported (reported, ignored):
  `@media`/`@keyframes`, `+ ~` and attribute selectors, `:not()`/`:first-child`, `gap`, `z-index` (use
  `ZOrder`), `border`/`font` shorthands, `box-shadow`, `cursor`.
- Reports are RETURNED, never raised: read `screen.Diagnostics` (`{Code, Severity, Source, Line, Column,
  Message}`) and fix the line. Raised errors carry a UI code: `UI_CAPABILITY_REQUIRED`, `UI_UNAVAILABLE`
  (no UI on this host: headless/server), `UI_DISABLED`, `UI_MOD_REQUIRED`, `UI_NOT_OWNER`,
  `UI_ELEMENT_DETACHED` (look the element up again; name what you keep), `UI_INVALID_SELECTOR`,
  `UI_UNSUPPORTED_OPERATION` (e.g. `Value` on a Label), `UI_INVALID_ARGUMENT`, and the per-mod budgets
  `UI_BUDGET_SCREENS` (32 screens), `UI_BUDGET_ELEMENTS` (4096 elements), `UI_TEXT_TOO_LARGE` (64 KB per
  call), `UI_BUDGET_WRITES` (8192 element writes per frame — write only what changed).

Hotbar, crosshair and block counter:
```lua
local UxmlService = game:GetService(""UxmlService"")
local UserInputService = game:GetService(""UserInputService"")

local UXML = [[
<ui:UXML xmlns:ui=""UnityEngine.UIElements"">
  <ui:Label name=""crosshair"" class=""crosshair"" text=""+"" picking-mode=""Ignore"" />
  <ui:VisualElement name=""hotbar"" class=""hotbar"">
    <ui:Button name=""slot1"" class=""slot"" text=""Stone"" />
    <ui:Button name=""slot2"" class=""slot"" text=""Dirt"" />
    <ui:Button name=""slot3"" class=""slot"" text=""Wood"" />
    <ui:Button name=""slot4"" class=""slot"" text=""Glass"" />
    <ui:Button name=""slot5"" class=""slot"" text=""Brick"" />
  </ui:VisualElement>
  <ui:Label name=""counter"" class=""counter"" text=""Blocks: 0"" />
</ui:UXML>
]]

local USS = [[
.hotbar { position: absolute; bottom: 16px; left: 50%; translate: -50% 0; flex-direction: row; }
.slot { width: 64px; height: 64px; margin: 4px; border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--cai-color-border);
        background-color: var(--cai-color-surface); color: var(--cai-color-text); }
.slot:hover { scale: 1.05; transition: scale 0.1s ease-out; }
.slot.is-selected { border-color: var(--cai-color-accent); border-width: 4px; }
.crosshair { position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; translate: -50% -50%; font-size: 28px; color: white; }
.counter { position: absolute; top: 16px; right: 16px; font-size: 20px; color: var(--cai-color-text); }
]]

local hud = UxmlService:CreateScreen(""hud"", UXML, USS, { SortOrder = 10 })
for _, report in ipairs(hud.Diagnostics) do
  warn(report.Code .. "" line "" .. report.Line .. "": "" .. report.Message)
end

local slots = hud:QueryAll(""#hotbar > .slot"")
local counter = hud:GetElement(""counter"")
local placed = 0

local function selectSlot(index)
  for i, slot in ipairs(slots) do
    if i == index then slot:AddClass(""is-selected"") else slot:RemoveClass(""is-selected"") end
  end
end

for i, slot in ipairs(slots) do
  slot.Clicked:Connect(function() selectSlot(i) end)
end

local keys = { Enum.KeyCode.One, Enum.KeyCode.Two, Enum.KeyCode.Three, Enum.KeyCode.Four, Enum.KeyCode.Five }
UserInputService.InputBegan:Connect(function(input, gameProcessed)
  if gameProcessed then return end
  if input.UserInputType == Enum.UserInputType.MouseButton1 then
    placed = placed + 1
    counter.Text = ""Blocks: "" .. placed
    return
  end
  for i, key in ipairs(keys) do
    if input.KeyCode == key then selectSlot(i) end
  end
end)

selectSlot(1)
```

<!-- section: chat -->
# TextChatService and TextChatCommand: slash commands for the in-world chat

`TextChatCommand` works as in Roblox. Parent one under `game:GetService(""TextChatService"")` (or a Folder
under it) with `PrimaryAlias` (`""/time""`; the slash is optional), an optional `SecondaryAlias` and
`Enabled` (default true). When the player sends a matching line, `Triggered(textSource, text)` fires with
the sender's `TextSource` (`UserId`; `Players:GetPlayerByUserId(textSource.UserId)` finds the player) and
the whole line (`""/time night""`), and the line never reaches the model. Where CoreAI differs:
- A command the chat already has keeps it: the game's own (`/undo`, `/fly`, `/agent`, ...) or another mod's
  earlier one. Yours is then not added at all (one log line); pick another alias.
- A command lives as long as its instance: it goes away with its mod, a reload adds it again, `Enabled =
  false` or moving it out of TextChatService removes it.
- A world without the in-world chat (a server, a headless tool) keeps the commands but never triggers them.
- The rest of TextChatService (`TextChannels`, `SendAsync`, `DisplaySystemMessage`, `MessageReceived`,
  `OnIncomingMessage`, bubbles) raises NOT_IMPLEMENTED (planned P2); `ChatVersion` is always
  `Enum.ChatVersion.TextChatService`. The legacy `Chat` service is unsupported.

```lua
local TextChatService = game:GetService(""TextChatService"")
local Lighting = game:GetService(""Lighting"")
local command = Instance.new(""TextChatCommand"")
command.Name = ""TimeCommand""
command.PrimaryAlias = ""/time""
command.SecondaryAlias = ""/t""
command.Triggered:Connect(function(textSource, text)
  local word = string.match(text, ""^%S+%s+(%a+)"")
  Lighting.ClockTime = (word == ""night"") and 0 or 12
end)
command.Parent = TextChatService
```

